WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:04.540
We were interested in making a
telescope that

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.149
anybody could download and and build
themselves. So

00:00:08.149 --> 00:00:11.700
obviously one of the things that we wanted to do was make it

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.070
3d-printable. So the idea was we'd use
one

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:19.800
screw and an allen key. and then you could literally just get the parts and then

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.490
construct it. The whole thing can be built
for about two hundred pounds.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:28.300
So the idea is to really make the
costs as low as possible.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.760
My name is James Parr. I'm the founder the open space agency

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.480
The open space agencies dedicated to
citizen science,

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.579
DIY engineering, the do it yourself

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:44.570
mentality and the belief that
everyday folks can

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:48.480
have their own space programme. We were increasingly interested in how

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.710
consumer and off-the-shelf technologies
were starting to approach the level of

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:56.809
pro technologies ten years ago so the
camera on

00:00:56.809 --> 00:01:00.780
NASA's Mars rover is essentially the
same quality

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.100
as a modern smartphone. The fact that
consumer technology is

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.920
now evolving so quickly made me
wonder whether

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.330
it was possible to do and replicate the achievements of

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:15.909
the space program using off-the-shelf
technology. And so do that we

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:19.250
decided to build the Ultrascope.
Something was really low cost

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.090
robotic, digital, had the ability to

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:26.420
look at objects from multiple
locations and combine those

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:30.900
observations in a way that was
meaningful and had scientific validity.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.860
As well as being a 3d-printed scope it's
also robot and

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:42.610
any robot has to have brains. So this
is the brain of it which is a

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:47.150
Arduino Mega and then we have our own
shield which basically runs the

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:51.030
motors and the drivers, and the
Bluetooth controller

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:55.770
and this just slots together like that. Then that slots onto the front of the scope

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.280
So we've got the the camera basically of the phone

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.810
which is how we take images the night
sky

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:07.619
and then the images that are taken on the
phone are then sent over 4G

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.989
to the cloud. Then we can see what we're
looking at here on our cloud app.

00:02:10.989 --> 00:02:15.749
And of course when we have hundreds
if not thousands of of these scopes around the world

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:19.390
then you will say I wonder what the
skies are going to be like in South Africa say

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:24.030
and you'll be able to dial up that scope and see what that scope's seeing.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:29.240
So I really love this time of the evening because you can really start to see the shadow of the earth rising

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:33.250
So it means we're not too far away from seeing some stars

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.590
Which just means it's time to reveal the mirror

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.500
So this is the primary mirror.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.939
When we get some objects to look, at the
light will come in here

00:02:43.939 --> 00:02:46.970
bounce off the primary mirror. Bounce up here

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:50.319
to the secondary mirror bounces the light

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:53.579
into the CCD of the smart phone.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.650
So when we started out the open space
agency felt

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:01.240
a bit like an art project or something
you know. It seemed so outlandish

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.480
that it was possible to have your own
space agency.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.530
Originally, the big rockets were the things
things that

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:11.300
governments could only do, and then business came along we're seeing that now.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.920
We're now in the domain of
personal space or citizen space.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.470
I think the open space agency has a
part to play

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:23.000
it really does. As we become a space-faring civilization

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.670
this so much work to do and I'd love for
OSA

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:31.570
to be there when we go to Mars. It might be that it has a NASA logo on the side of the spaceship

00:03:31.769 --> 00:03:35.890
but you know for some of the experiments to be OSA experiments or

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:40.500
perhaps some of the technology that the astronauts use in their space suits

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:41.870
it might not be branded but

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:46.519
it'll be there and we'll know as a bunch
of geeks that

00:03:46.519 --> 00:03:48.919
we helped get there

